<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Poland, Lithuania and Russia</p><h1 class="st-title">Principality of Turov and Pinsk</h1><p class="st-meta">1157 CE – 1320 CE (approx.) · capital Turov</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A principality of the Pripyat marshes in southern Belarus, known for the preacher Cyril of Turov.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turov">Turov</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pripyat-river">Pripyat</a> had been an early seat of Rus' princes, ruled by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sviatopolk-i-of-kiev">Sviatopolk</a>, son of Vladimir I, in the late 10th century. It was long attached to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a>, but in the late 1150s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yuri-yaroslavich-of-turov">Yuri Yaroslavich</a>, a grandson of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sviatopolk-ii-of-kiev">Sviatopolk II</a>, made it independent, founding a local dynasty.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The principality covered the wet, wooded lands of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/dregovichs">Dregovichs</a> and lived from farming, fishing and river trade. Its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyril-of-turov">Cyril of Turov</a>, who died around 1182, was one of the finest preachers and writers of early Rus'. Over time it split into smaller <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/appanage">appanages</a> based on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turov">Turov</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pinsk">Pinsk</a> and other towns, and its princes became minor players.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After the Mongol invasion its lands weakened further, and in the early 14th century they were absorbed by the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-lithuania">Grand Duchy of Lithuania</a> under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gediminas">Gediminas</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pinsk">Pinsk</a> later became a Lithuanian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/appanage">appanage</a> duchy.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/principality-of-turov-and-pinsk.jpg" alt="Principality of Turov and Pinsk" width="320" height="263"><figcaption>Image: Goran tek-en, following request by and knowledge from Veverve · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:1237_Kievan_Rus%27_Livonia_Lithuania.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1157 CE – 1320 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Turov</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Yuri Yaroslavich</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Eastern Orthodox Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old East Slavic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/belarus">Belarus</a>, <a href="/countries/ukraine">Ukraine</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Principality of Turov, Turov-Pinsk</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1238&amp;focus=principality-of-turov-and-pinsk" aria-label="Principality of Turov and Pinsk on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5305 379 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Principality of Turov and Pinsk lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5625 635L5697 681M5625 635L5556 562"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5697 681h0M5556 562h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5625 635h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5625 635h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Turov. <a href="/atlas?year=1238&amp;focus=principality-of-turov-and-pinsk">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
